            {
              "given": "Zhaolin",
              "family": "Chen",
              "sequence": "additional",
              "affiliation": [],
              "role": [
                {
                  "role": "author",
                  "vocabulary": "crossref"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "published": null,
          "abstract": "<jats:p>Deep learning (DL) models for accelerated image reconstruction involves retrospective undersampling of the fully sampled k-space data for training and validation. This strategy is not a true reflection of real-world data and in many instances, the input k-space data is corrupted with artifacts and errors, such as motion artifacts. In this work, we propose to improve existing methods of DL training and validation by incorporating a motion layer during the training process. The incorporation of a motion layer makes the DL model aware of the underlying motion and results in improved image reconstruction in the presence of motion.</jats:p>",
          "link": null,
          "URL": "https://doi.org/10.58530/2022/4691"
        },
